# The build context for apps/worker/Dockerfile.
#
# The first four lines are the load-bearing ones, and they are load-bearing for different reasons.
#
# **Environment files.** `apps/worker/Dockerfile` does `COPY . .`, so without this the build context carries
# whatever `.env` a developer has locally, and an image layer is immutable and readable by anyone who can
# pull it — a credential copied in cannot be removed by a later layer. `scripts/check-container.mjs` checks
# both halves of this: `[env-file-copied-into-image]` on a COPY that names one, and
# `[dockerignore-does-not-exclude-env]` on a Dockerfile that copies the whole context without this file
# excluding them. The second rule exists because the first one cannot see `COPY . .`.
#
# **node_modules.** pnpm's tree is a forest of symlinks into `node_modules/.pnpm`, and copying a host one
# into the image would land links pointing at paths that do not exist there. The image resolves its own.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output and generated artefacts. None of it is input to the worker, and `.next` alone is most of a
# gigabyte of chunks.
.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
artifacts
playwright-report
test-results
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# Git history, worktrees and editor state. `.claude/worktrees` is a parallel checkout of this same
# repository — copying it would put every other agent's work in progress into the image.
.git
.gitignore
.claude
.DS_Store
